// An empty production at the end of what it is part of. This is where the span of a node is decided by what it leaves
// out: a terminator is a semicolon or nothing at all, and an item whose terminator is missing ends at its own last
// byte. The ε-node sits where the parser stands when it reduces, which is the start of the token it is looking at, so
// the whitespace between two items belongs to neither of them.
//
// Both places a trailing ε-node can sit are in the input: the first item is followed by another one, so its ε-node
// sits at the start of that item, and the last item is followed by the end of the input, so its ε-node sits one past
// the last byte. Neither widens the item it belongs to.
//
// The other end of this is covered by empty-production, where the ε-node is the first child of its production and the
// parse opens with a reduction before anything at all has been shifted.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    ITEM:      "i";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    list
        : list item
        | item
        ;

    item
        : ITEM terminator
        ;

    terminator
        : SEMICOLON
        | @empty
        ;
}
